      "message": "Two notes on `reproduces`:\n\n- **What it can\u0027t tell apart.** A crafted artifact can add a scope of its own that binds none of its definitions, so it isn\u0027t in `own_scopes`, and bind the other library\u0027s private variable under it. That maps one to one onto the owner\u0027s entry exactly as a real import\u0027s remapped scope does, and its templates can carry that scope. The loader can\u0027t tell a fabricated scope from a remapped one. That\u0027s fine given #9\u0027s last sentence: the artifact could link the variable from its code anyway. It\u0027s worth one line in the doc comment, so nobody later leans on this as more than it is.\n- **Cost.** Each entry that names another library\u0027s private variable scans that library\u0027s whole table, and clones the trial map per candidate. A library that imports `(scheme base)` carries a remapped copy of base\u0027s table, which is mostly its private definitions, so loading its artifact does about entries × entries comparisons. Indexing each owner\u0027s table once by `(symbol, name)` would make it linear. Not urgent, but every library load pays it.",
